 Geometric properties of the problem can be considered to find the radius of the sphere and its center coordinates. Let's consider half of the sphere. It's good to choose one of the vertices of the cube, for example, point A(1,1,1).

 Center of the sphere will be one of the corner points of its cover. Its edges will be segments AC and AB. Coordinates of points A and C are (1,1,1) and (1,0,0). 

On the other hand, coordinates of point B are (1,0,0). Let's find out what kind of shape we are dealing with. Short side AB and side AC in that triangle are lines with coordinates (1,0,-1)(1,0,1) and (1,-1,0)(1,1,0). 

It turns out that all three segments are unit length and their directions can be made to coincide. In this case the triangle becomes a simple equilateral triangle inscribed in half of a sphere. Its center coincides with the center of the sphere. Therefore, radius of the sphere is $1$; coordinates of the center: $(0,0,\sqrt 2/2)$.

To prove that sphere is tangent to axes, consider it once more. It is clear that a projection of the sphere's diameter to the axis cuts the axis at right angle. Furthermore, all three points, where the diameter touches axes will be equidistant from this point and the points c and d (c is the first projection of the point B, and d is the first projection of the point A). Hence these points lie on the same half-circle on sphere and do go on one arc. The same can be said for their predecessors. This means that sphere cuts both coordinate axes at points that lie symmetrically with respect to their intersection with the respective axis. All semi-axes  (projected onto coordinate axes) of a sphere, begin at point, where the largest of them coincides with origin.

